==Russian==
{{wp|ru:ё}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{m|ru|е}}

===Etymology===
Stressed {{IPAchar|[ʲe]}} regularly developed into {{IPAchar|[ʲɵ]}} before hard consonants. The letter {{m|ru||ё}} was later created to reflect this, the [[diaeresis]] probably being inspired by the German use of diaeresis for [[umlaut]]. Its first appearance in typography was in 1797, introduced by the writer {{w|Nikolay Karamzin}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA|ё}} {{audio|ru|Ru-07-буква-Ё.ogg}}

===Letter===
{{letter|ru}}

# {{Cyrl-def|ru|letter|7|ё}}

====Usage notes====
* This letter almost invariably bears the word stress.
* Its use is optional as it is equally accepted and common to write simple {{m|ru|е}}. While {{m|ru||ё}} is always used in dictionaries and children’s books, other usage varies and depends on the preferences of the writer and/or publisher.
* When {{IPAchar|[ʲɵ]}}, {{IPAchar|[jɵ]}} do not go back to etymological {{m|ru||е}}, they are normally spelt {{m|ru||ьо}} and {{m|ru||йо}}, as in {{m|ru|батальон}}, {{m|ru|район}}. {{m|ru||ьо}} and {{m|ru||йо}} are also used to transcribe /jo/ (yo) in other languages, such as {{m|ru|Нью-Йорк||New York}}. However, {{m|ru||ё}} is used to represent the front-rounded vowels {{IPAchar|/ø/|/œ/}} of languages like French and German, as in {{m|ru|Кёльн}}.

